Mary then departs for Judea to visit Elizabeth, who, when she hears the greeting from Mary feels the baby in her womb leap for joy. As God had promised, the Holy Ghost would be upon John, and here He comes upon Elizabeth as well, who hearing Mary's story, begins to prophesy;

*Blessed is she that believed: for there shall be a performance of those things which were told her from the Lord*.

Mary is blessed among women because she believed. The blessing is from God. She is "full of grace," not because she is special, deserving, or "strong in the force," or anything like that. Grace is a gift unearned, given by God. Also overtaken by the Holy Ghost, Mary replies to Elizabeth;

*My soul doth magnify the Lord, And my spirit hath rejoiced in God my Saviour. For he hath regarded the low estate of his handmaiden: for, behold, from henceforth all generations shall call me blessed. For he that is mighty hath done to me great things; and holy is his name.*

You see the emphasis here is always on God and His handiwork. Not on Mary for being the vessel. And God is faithful and true, keeping His promises;

*As he spake to our fathers, to Abraham, and to his seed for ever.*
